<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<policyDefinitionResources revision="1.0" schemaVersion="1.0"
                           xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/GroupPolicy/2006/07/PolicyDefinitions">

  <displayName>OneDriveAsADrive</displayName>
  <description>Administrative template for OneDriveAsADrive, the local WebDAV bridge that mounts OneDrive and SharePoint document libraries as drive letters.</description>

  <resources>
    <stringTable>

      <string id="SUPPORTED_ODAD_1_3">OneDriveAsADrive 1.3 or later</string>

      <string id="Cat_OneDriveAsADrive">OneDriveAsADrive</string>
      <string id="Cat_OneDriveAsADrive_Help">Settings for OneDriveAsADrive.

None of these settings are required. If you configure nothing here, users manage their own port and drives from the built-in settings page, and any config.json you deploy to %ProgramData%\OneDriveAsADrive acts as a starting point that users may change.

Configure a setting here only when you want to remove that choice from the user. Anything you set below is enforced: the settings page shows it as managed by your organization and refuses to change it.</string>

      <string id="Port">Local port for the WebDAV server</string>
      <string id="Port_Help">Fixes the loopback port that OneDriveAsADrive listens on, and prevents users from changing it.

The server only ever binds to localhost, so this port is not reachable from the network. Set it when a conflicting local application already owns the default (40323), or when your endpoint tooling needs a predictable port.

If you enable this policy, the port is fixed at the value you supply and the settings page shows it as managed.

If you disable or do not configure this policy, the port comes from the user's own setting, then from a deployed machine-wide config.json, then from the built-in default of 40323.

Changing this policy takes effect the next time OneDriveAsADrive starts. Mapped drives embed the port, so users need to re-map their drives afterwards - the Re-map drives button on the settings page does this, or re-run the installer.</string>

      <string id="Account">Sign-in account</string>
      <string id="Account_Help">Pins the user principal name that OneDriveAsADrive signs in with, and prevents users from changing it.

Use this on machines where people are signed in to more than one identity (for example a personal Microsoft account alongside their work account) and you want the drives bound to the work account.

Supply a UPN such as user@contoso.com. Windows environment variables are not expanded; if you need per-user values, target the policy per user or leave this unconfigured.

If you disable or do not configure this policy, OneDriveAsADrive uses the account the user chose, or the first account the Windows broker has cached.

Changing this policy takes effect the next time OneDriveAsADrive starts.</string>

      <string id="Mounts">Drives to mount</string>
      <string id="Mounts_Help">Defines the exact set of drives every targeted user gets, and prevents users from adding, removing, or editing them.

Supply a JSON array in the same shape as the mounts array in config.json, on a single line. For example:

[{"letter":"O","type":"onedrive","name":"OneDrive"},{"letter":"S","type":"sharepoint","site":"contoso.sharepoint.com:/sites/Finance","library":"Documents","name":"Finance"}]

Each entry needs a single drive letter and a type of either onedrive or sharepoint. SharePoint entries also need a site, and may name a library (the default document library is used if you omit it). Entries that fail validation are dropped and logged.

This is the strictest of the drive settings. If you only want to stop users changing a list you deployed through config.json, leave this unconfigured and disable "Allow users to manage their own drives" instead.

If you disable or do not configure this policy, the drive list comes from the user's own setting, then from a deployed machine-wide config.json, then from the built-in default of a single OneDrive on Z:.

Adding a SharePoint drive through this policy widens the Microsoft Graph permissions the app requests, which may need a one-time tenant admin consent, and takes effect the next time OneDriveAsADrive starts.</string>

      <string id="AllowUserMounts">Allow users to manage their own drives</string>
      <string id="AllowUserMounts_Help">Controls whether users may add, remove, or edit drives on the settings page.

Use this together with a config.json deployed to %ProgramData%\OneDriveAsADrive when you want to hand out a standard set of drives and keep it that way, without having to restate the whole list as a policy.

If you enable or do not configure this policy, users may manage their own drives. This is the default.

If you disable this policy, the drive list is whatever the deployed config.json says, and the settings page shows it as managed. Users can still see their drives and re-map them; they cannot change which drives exist.

The port and the account are unaffected by this policy. Set those separately if you also want them fixed.</string>

      <string id="DisableSettingsUi">Turn off the settings page</string>
      <string id="DisableSettingsUi_Help">Removes the built-in settings page entirely.

With this enabled, the local settings endpoint stops answering and OneDriveAsADrive.exe --settings refuses to open a browser. Users get no interface at all - the app runs on whatever configuration you deployed.

Prefer the narrower settings above where they will do. Turning off the page also removes the sign-in button and the re-map button, so users who hit an expired token or a broken drive mapping have no self-service path and will call your service desk instead.

If you enable this policy, the settings page is unavailable.

If you disable or do not configure this policy, the settings page is available on the loopback interface to the signed-in user. This is the default.

The page is never reachable from the network: it binds to localhost only, requires a token derived from a per-user secret file to start a session, and rejects cross-origin requests.</string>

    </stringTable>

    <presentationTable>

      <presentation id="Port">
        <decimalTextBox refId="Port" defaultValue="40323" spinStep="1">Port (1024-65535):</decimalTextBox>
      </presentation>

      <presentation id="Account">
        <textBox refId="Account">
          <label>User principal name:</label>
        </textBox>
      </presentation>

      <presentation id="Mounts">
        <textBox refId="Mounts">
          <label>Mounts (JSON array, single line):</label>
        </textBox>
      </presentation>

    </presentationTable>
  </resources>
</policyDefinitionResources>
